`z.compile`: a runtime island leaks one indent level into the rest of the generated source, leading to substantial memory overhead

Compiling a large schema that contains runtime islands generates source that is mostly indentation, and that source stays resident in memory.

On a real 162-branch discriminated union in our codebase with 212 `z.coerce.number()` islands, `z.compile` emits 34.1 MB of source of which 29.7 MB is leading whitespace, and the process retains 68 MB more heap on Node 24 (35 MB on Node 26) than before the compile. With the one-line fix below the same schema compiles to 5.5 MB of source and retains 13 MB.

`Doc.indented()` restores the indent level only when the callback returns normally:

```ts
indented(fn: (doc: Doc) => void) {
  this.indent += 1;
  fn(this);
  this.indent -= 1;
}
```

When a child generator throws `ZodCompileUnsupportedError` inside an indented block, `compileChild` catches it and emits a runtime island, but the decrement never runs. Every line emitted after that point carries one extra indent level, and each further island adds another. Nothing reads the whitespace, but on Node 24 the generated source is retained with the function (see #6464), so it is live memory.

### Repro

(zod 4.5.4, Node 24.14 and 26.8)

```ts
import * as z from "zod";
import { compileFn } from "zod/v4/core";

const schema = z.object({ a: z.coerce.number().nullable() });
console.log(compileFn(schema, { debug: true }).code);
```

`nullable` opens an `if (x !== null) {` block, `z.coerce.number()` throws inside it, and everything after the island is shifted right:

```js
// Constants: INVALID, c0, c1
if (typeof input !== "object" || input === null || Array.isArray(input)) return INVALID;
const v0 = input["a"];
if (!("a" in input)) return INVALID;
  const v1 = c1(c0, v0);
  if (v1 === INVALID) return INVALID;
  const v2 = { "a": v1 };
  return v2;
```

### Fix

Move the decrement into a `finally`:

```diff
   indented(fn: (doc: Doc) => void) {
     this.indent += 1;
-    fn(this);
-    this.indent -= 1;
+    try {
+      fn(this);
+    } finally {
+      this.indent -= 1;
+    }
   }
```

Branch with the change and an inline-snapshot test on the repro above, passing the full suite (linked since it seems I'm not permitted to contribute a pull request due to current repo configuration): https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/compare/main...aaronhipple:zod:fix/compile-doc-indent-leak?expand=1

`toJSONSchema()`: chaining `.int()` after `.min()`/`.max()` replaces explicit bounds

Calling `.int()` after explicit numeric constraints changes the generated JSON Schema, even though runtime validation behaves identically.

Tested with:

- Zod 4.5.4
- Node.js 24.18.0
- JSON Schema Draft 2020-12 and OpenAPI 3.0 targets

### Minimal reproduction

```ts
import * as z from "zod";

const intFirst = z.number().int().min(0).max(23);
const intLast = z.number().min(0).max(23).int();

for (const target of ["draft-2020-12", "openapi-3.0"] as const) {
  console.log(target);
  console.log("intFirst", z.toJSONSchema(intFirst, { target }));
  console.log("intLast", z.toJSONSchema(intLast, { target }));
}
```

### Actual output

For both targets:

```ts
// z.number().int().min(0).max(23)
{
  type: "integer",
  minimum: 0,
  maximum: 23
}

// z.number().min(0).max(23).int()
{
  type: "integer",
  minimum: -9007199254740991,
  maximum: 9007199254740991
}
```

Both schemas still behave identically during runtime validation:

- `-1` is rejected
- `0` is accepted
- `1.5` is rejected
- `23` is accepted
- `24` is rejected

### Expected output

The explicit `minimum: 0` and `maximum: 23` constraints should be preserved regardless of whether `.int()` appears before or after them.

At minimum, the generated schema should not describe different accepted ranges when the two Zod schemas accept the same values.

This matters when `toJSONSchema()` is used to produce an OpenAPI contract: applying `.int()` last causes the published contract to lose the application's explicit range.

### Related issue

[#4779](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/4779) discusses the default safe-integer range emitted for an unconstrained integer. This case is different because explicit, tighter constraints are present and enforced at runtime, but are replaced only in the generated schema when `.int()` is applied last.

Stop building pattern regexes eagerly

AI generated proposal to improve performance for @pullfrog to evaluate:

z.string(), z.literal() and z.enum() each compile a RegExp at construction, but _zod.pattern is only ever read by z.templateLiteral(), toJSONSchema() and union pattern derivation. Literal and enum now build it lazily through the existing defineLazyInternal, which also drops the escapeRegex/map/join work from the constructor. For strings the pattern stays eager but an unconstrained bag returns one shared regex instead of a fresh one, and the [...patterns].pop() array is gone.

Impact: construction z.enum() −44.3%, z.literal() −24.2%, z.string() −25.5%, discriminated unions −15.9%. Memory −1.26% on a realistic catalogue (−88 B per string schema, −91 B per literal, −100 B per enum). Parse unchanged. Bundle +63 B gzip classic, +49 B mini — the string half accounts for nearly all of that; the literal/enum half is free on mini because defineLazyInternal is already there.

My proposed change:

```diff
diff --git a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/regexes.ts b/packages/zod/src/v4/core/regexes.ts
index 4713681d..64a8beb5 100644
--- a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/regexes.ts
+++ b/packages/zod/src/v4/core/regexes.ts
@@ -147,7 +147,11 @@ export function datetime(args: {
   return new RegExp(`^${dateSource}T(?:${timeRegex})$`);
 }

+// what an unconstrained bag builds; shared so a bare z.string() allocates no regex of its own
+const anyString: RegExp = /^[\s\S]{0,}$/;
+
 export const string = (params?: { minimum?: number | undefined; maximum?: number | undefined }): RegExp => {
+  if (params && params.minimum === undefined && params.maximum === undefined) return anyString;
   const regex = params ? `[\\s\\S]{${params?.minimum ?? 0},${params?.maximum ?? ""}}` : `[\\s\\S]*`;
   return new RegExp(`^${regex}$`);
 };
diff --git a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts b/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts
index 893ff942..b45dc9b1 100644
--- a/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts
