`toZod<T>()` rejects enum schema for enum-typed property, but accepts equivalent union of enum members

`z.toZod<T>()` accepts schemas when a property is typed as a union of enum members:
`someEnum: SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B;`
However, when the property is typed as the enum itself:
`someEnum: SomeEnum;`
The corresponding Zod schemas are rejected by TypeScript, even though they represent the same set of enum values.

### Environment

- `typescript@6.0.3`
- `zod@4.5.4`

### Reproduction

When the interface property is a **union of enum members**, the type check passes:

```ts
enum SomeEnum {
  A = "A",
  B = "B",
}

interface SomeObject {
  someEnum: SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B;
}

const schema = z.toZod<SomeObject>()(
  z.object({
    someEnum: z.enum(SomeEnum),
    // also works:
    // someEnum: z.enum([SomeEnum.A, SomeEnum.B]),
    // someEnum: z.union([z.literal(SomeEnum.A), z.literal(SomeEnum.B)]),
  }),
);
```

However, when the interface property is typed as the **enum itself**, the same schemas are rejected:

```ts
enum SomeEnum {
  A = "A",
  B = "B",
}

interface SomeObject {
  someEnum: SomeEnum;
}

const schema = z.toZod<SomeObject>()(
  z.object({
    someEnum: z.enum(SomeEnum),
    // same failure with:
    // someEnum: z.enum([SomeEnum.A, SomeEnum.B]),
    // someEnum: z.union([z.literal(SomeEnum.A), z.literal(SomeEnum.B)]),
  }),
);
```

### TypeScript error

```txt
Argument of type 'ZodObject<{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }, $strip>' is not assignable to parameter of type '$ZodObject<{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }, $strip>'.
  Types of property '_zod' are incompatible.
    Type '$ZodObjectInternals<{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }, $strip>' is not assignable to type '$ZodObjectInternals<{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }, $strip>'.
      Type '{ someEnum: ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>; }' is not assignable to type '{ someEnum: ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>; }'.
        Types of property 'someEnum' are incompatible.
          Type 'ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>' is not assignable to type 'ToZodKeyMismatch<SomeEnum, SomeEnum>'.
            Property '"types do not match"' is missing in type 'ZodUnion<readonly [ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.A>, ZodLiteral<SomeEnum.B>]>' but required in type '{ "types do not match": { expected: SomeEnum; received: SomeEnum; }; }'.ts(2345)
util.d.cts(25, 5): '"types do not match"' is declared here.
```

### Expected behavior

`toZod<SomeObject>()` should accept a schema when its inferred output type is compatible with the corresponding property type.

In particular, a schema representing all members of `SomeEnum` should be accepted for a property typed as `SomeEnum`, just as the equivalent union of enum members is accepted.

### Actual behavior

The same set of enum values is accepted when the property is typed as `SomeEnum.A | SomeEnum.B`, but rejected when the property is typed as `SomeEnum`.

### Notes

This appears to be a type-level compatibility issue in `toZod`'s handling of enum types, rather than a runtime validation issue.

Related discussion: https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/pull/5913#issuecomment-5370100533

Functions are executed twice for object property defaults

If you define an object type and use a function for a property's default value, the function is executed twice:
```js
let test = 0;
const schema = z.object({

    TEST: z.string().default(() => {
        console.log(`Executed... ${test++}`);
        return 'default';
    })

})

schema.parse({});
// Executed... 0
// Executed... 1
```

When it's not wrapped in an object, the function is only executed once, as expected:
```js
const defaultTuna = z.string().default(() => {
    console.log(`tuna`);
    return 'tuna';
});

defaultTuna.parse(undefined); // => "tuna"
```

I'm currently using Node.js v24.14.1 and Zod v4.5.2. 
I have not checked the versions in between, but Zod v4.4.2 does not have this issue and the default function is only executed once.

`toJSONSchema` on record with numeric keys results in invalid `propertyNames`

This code with Zod 4.5.2:

```
import * as z from "zod";

const schema = z.record(z.number(), z.number());
const jsonSchema = z.toJSONSchema(schema);
console.log(jsonSchema);
```

produces this output:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  propertyNames: { type: 'number' },
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

which can never work because in JSON the property names are always strings.

Similar issue in TypeScript with `Record<number, number>` being kind of weird, but Zod already handles that correctly (as [the docs](https://zod.dev/api?id=zrecord#zrecord) say, "Zod supports numeric keys inside records in a way that closely mirrors TypeScript itself"). So for `toJSONSchema` wouldn't it make sense to also support numeric keys in a sensible way?

The output might be better as:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  propertyNames: { type: 'string' },
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

But that's redundant since property names are always strings, so:

```
{
  '$schema': 'https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema',
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: { type: 'number' }
}
```

(You could imagine  doing something like `"propertyNames": { "pattern": "^[0-9]+$" }` but idk if that's worth the trouble because it'd have to be a fairly complex regex to handle negative numbers, decimals, NaN, Infinity, maybe more?)

Distinguish different number formats in type-level

Currently the situation at the type-level is following:

```typescript
export interface ZodInt extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodFloat32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodFloat64 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export interface ZodUInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {}
export declare function int(...): ZodInt;
export declare function float32(...): ZodFloat32;
export declare function float64(...): ZodFloat64;
export declare function int32(...): ZodInt32;
export declare function uint32(...): ZodUInt32;
```

Also same with ZodBigIntFormat, they do have string-literal types but no ability to narrow, since both return same type:

```typescript
export declare function int64(...): ZodBigIntFormat;
export declare function uint64(...): ZodBigIntFormat;
```

This makes it impossible to use these to for instance derive Postgres types which are required to exist typed at type-level too (for Kysely etc).

## Suggestion

What I would like them to be is something like:

```typescript
export interface ZodInt extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "safeint"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodFloat32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "float32"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodFloat64 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "float64"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "int32"
    }
  }
}
export interface ZodUInt32 extends ZodNumberFormat {
  _zod: {
    def: {
      format: "uint32"
    }
  }
}
```

This would allow me to use that format (which already exists in runtime) as differentiator.

Thanks.

Wrong input type inferred from record with enum keys and default values

When defining a Record with enums as keys and values with defaults, then zod is able to complete the missing keys with the default values in the parsed result:
```ts
import z from "zod"

const schema = z.record(z.enum(["UP", "DOWN"]), z.string().default("unknown"))

type Input = z.input<typeof schema>

// @ts-ignore
const input: Input = {
    UP: "somewhere"
}

console.log(z.parse(schema, input))
```
output:
```
{ UP: 'somewhere', DOWN: 'unknown' }
```

The only issue is that the inferred type `Input` is:
```ts
type Input = {
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}
```
which requires using `// @ts-ignore` when defining partial inputs, otherwise typescript complains with:
```
error TS2741: Property 'DOWN' is missing in type '{ UP: string; }' but required in type 'Record<"UP" | "DOWN", string | undefined>
```

Assuming it is intended for zod to generate the missing fields in the output, then it would be amazing if the inferred `z.input` type to be Partial. For example:
```ts
type Input = Partial<{
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}>
```

Properties with Symbol keys are stripped from objects, even with passthrough()

TL;DR Properties with symbol keys are stripped from objects regardless of the object shape.  

`ZodRawShape` is defined as `[k: string]: ZodTypeAny` but according to Typescript rules, this [does not mean that a passed object should only have string keys](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/53265).  I think this is why Zod allows the object shape but then doesn't handle the presence of Symbol keys.

Therefore there should probably, at least, be a runtime exception to indicate that they are not handled.

```ts
const bar = Symbol('bar')

const Schema = z.object({
  foo: z.string(),
  [bar]: z.string()
})

const input: z.infer<typeof Schema> = {
  foo: 'foo',
  [bar]: 'bar',
}

const output = Schema.parse(input)
```

Results in:

```js
{
  foo: 'foo'
}
```

It seems that symbol keys are ignored in the object specification, as `z.string().default('bar')` also does not result in there being a property for `Symbol(bar)` with value `bar` in the output.

This also happens if the symbol property is included or omitted, and the schema has `.passthrough()` added.

Typecheck schemas against existing types

### TL;DR
I would love to have a way to ensure that a Zod schema matches an existing type definition using the normal Typescript type checker. Below is an example of the desired functionality using one possible implementation stolen from [this comment](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/53#issuecomment-655879215).

```typescript
type Dog = {
  name: string
  neutered: boolean
}

//Passing "Dog" as a generic type parameter tells Typescript what the schema should look like
const dogSchema = z.object<Dog>({
  name: z.string().min(3),
  neutered: z.string(), //Error: string can't be assigned to boolean
});
```

### Introduction

I originally raised this suggestion in #53, but decided it probably needs its own issue. See my original comments [here](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/53#issuecomment-737550649) and [here](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/53#issuecomment-738110750). Based on the reactions to these comments, there are at least a few other people thinking along the same lines as I am. I will restate my thoughts below.

I want to start by saying that Zod is a really, really cool project. It is the best runtime validation system for typescript by far. I hope the following critiques are constructive. 

### My runtime validation requirements

I started implementing Zod in my project, and I went into this implementation assuming that Zod would meet the following two requirements:

1.  Zod schemas would provide run time checks of my data types. **[true]**
2.  Zod schemas would conform to my existing types, so that it is impossible to change the type without also changing the associated schema (and vice versa) . **[only sort of true]**

In order to get the effect of my second requirement, I discovered that I need to replace my existing code, eg...

```typescript
export interface A {
   readonly ID: number;
   delayEnd: number;
   userID: number;
   reason: string;
   taskID: number;
   initiationDate: number;
   days?: number;
   userName?: string;
}
```

...with something like this...

```typescript
export const aSchema = z.object({
   ID: z.number(), //Note that I've lost the functionality of `readonly` in this conversion
   delayEnd: z.number(),
   userID: z.number(),
   reason: z.string(),
   taskID: z.number(),
   initiationDate: z.number(),
   days: z.number().optional(),
   userName: z.string().optional()
});

//"A" is generated from the schema
export type A = z.infer<typeof aSchema>;
```

