Specification of `*` and '/*' in routing

I think the top-level `*` and `/*` behave a little differently than the documentation.

https://github.com/honojs/hono/releases/tag/v1.4.0
```typescript
app.get('/', (c) => {
  return c.text('foo')
}) // <--- stop process

app.use('*', middleware()) // <--- not dispached!!
```

Currently, in both TrieRouter and RegExpRouter, `*` and `/*` have higher precedence than `/`.
( On TrieRouter, `*` has the highest priority, `/*` the next highest; on RegExpRouter, `*` and `/*` have the same priority. )

```typescript
describe('star', () => {
  const router = new TrieRouter<string>()

  router.add('GET', '/', '/')
  router.add('GET', '/*', '/*')
  router.add('GET', '*', '*')

  router.add('GET', '/x', '/x')
  router.add('GET', '/x/*', '/x/*')

  it.only('top', async () => {
    const res = router.match('GET', '/')
    expect(res).not.toBeNull()
    expect(res.handlers).toEqual(['*', '/*', '/'])
  })

  it.only('Under a certain path', async () => {
    const res = router.match('GET', '/x')
    expect(res).not.toBeNull()
    expect(res.handlers).toEqual(['*', '/*', '/x', '/x/*'])
  })
})
```

```typescript
describe('star', () => {
  const router = new RegExpRouter<string>()

  router.add('GET', '/', '/')
  router.add('GET', '/*', '/*')
  router.add('GET', '*', '*')

  router.add('GET', '/x', '/x')
  router.add('GET', '/x/*', '/x/*')

  it.only('top', async () => {
    const res = router.match('GET', '/')
    expect(res).not.toBeNull()
    expect(res.handlers).toEqual(['*', '/*', '/']) // => failed ['/*', '*', '/']
  })

  it.only('Under a certain path', async () => {
    const res = router.match('GET', '/x')
    expect(res).not.toBeNull()
    expect(res.handlers).toEqual(['*', '/*', '/x', '/x/*'])
  })
})
```

The scores appear to be as follows.

at TrieRouter

```typescript
router.add('GET', '/', '/')  // score: 1.1
router.add('GET', '/*', '/*') // score: 0.2
router.add('GET', '*', '*') // score: 0.3 - 1 => Approx. -0.7
```

at RegExpRouter

```typescript
router.add('GET', '/', '/')  // score: 1.1
router.add('GET', '/*', '/*') // score: 0.2 in RegExpRouter "/*" is an alias to "*".
router.add('GET', '*', '*') // score: 0.3
```

I think the current specification for TrieRouter is acceptable to us as users, so I can make it the official specification and adapt RegExpRouter to it.
Alternatively, we can treat '/*' as an alias to '*', which would be the same as the RegExpRouter implementation.

