`invalidateQueries` does not work as expected

Welcome back @KATT, I hope you enjoyed your holidays 😀

The [`invalidateQueries`](https://trpc.io/docs/invalidateQueries) docs say:

> [See react-query docs](https://react-query.tanstack.com/guides/query-invalidation) if you want more fine-grained control.

In turn, the react query docs say:

- `invalidateQueries()` will invalidate every query (I found this on some other page)
- `invalidateQueries('todos')` will invalidate every query with a key that starts with `'todos'`
- `invalidateQueries({ predicate: query => boolean })` allows fine-grain control

However, if you now try the following with trpc:

```ts
import { trpc } from '../utils/trpc'

const context = trpc.useContext()

// Invalid
context.invalidateQueries()

// Works differently than expected:
//   Will invalidate the exact query 'todos'
//   Will not clear queries that start with 'todos'!
context.invalidateQueries('todos') 

// Invalid
context.invalidateQueries({ predicate: () => true })

const { queryClient } = context

// Doesn't seem to work for me. Does nothing.
queryClient.invalidateQueries('todos')

// Will invalidate the exact query 'todos', if it has no params
queryClient.invalidateQueries(['todos'])
```

If we want to invalidate all queries it must be done as so:

```ts
queryClient.invalidateQueries()
```

If we want to invalidate queries that start with `'todos'` it must be done like so:

```ts
queryClient.invalidateQueries({
  predicate({ queryKey }) {
    const name = queryKey[0]
    return typeof name === 'string' && name.startsWith('todos')
  },
})
```

For example, when a user logs off in my application, I clear all private queries like so:

```ts
queryClient.removeQueries({
  predicate({ queryKey }) {
    const name = queryKey[0]
    return typeof name !== 'string' || !name.includes('public')
  },
})
```

Solutions I propose:

1. The distinction should be made in the docs that trpc uses arrays as keys, not strings, and thus `invalidateQueries` can't be used in certain ways.

2. The typing of `invalidateQueries` needs to be fixed (at least) to allow empty args and `{ predicate: (query) => boolean }`.

