### Describe the bug

With a `projects` config containing a browser-mode project, the `--maxWorkers` CLI flag has no observable effect on how many browser tabs run in parallel. Setting the same value as `maxWorkers` inside the project's `test` config **does** work.

The flag is accepted without warning, so it silently does nothing. I went looking for a way to cap browser concurrency on a loaded CI box, concluded from the CLI that `maxWorkers` was simply not honoured in browser mode, and only found out otherwise after reading the source.

### Reproduction

A browser project in a `projects` config is enough:

```ts
// vitest.config.ts
import { playwright } from "@vitest/browser-playwright";
import { defineConfig } from "vitest/config";

export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    projects: [
      {
        extends: true,
        test: {
          name: "ui",
          include: ["src/**/*.test.tsx"],
          browser: {
            enabled: true,
            headless: true,
            provider: playwright({}),
            instances: [{ browser: "chromium" }],
          },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

Then compare, on a suite with enough files to show parallelism (mine has 122 files / 609 tests):

```sh
vitest run --project ui                       # baseline
vitest run --project ui --maxWorkers=1        # expected: serial
# then set `maxWorkers: 1` inside the project's test config and run again
vitest run --project ui
```

### Expected behaviour

`--maxWorkers=1` produces the same single-tab, serial execution as `maxWorkers: 1` in the project config.

### Actual behaviour

The CLI flag changes nothing; only the config value does.

| how `maxWorkers: 1` is applied | duration |
| --- | --- |
| not set (baseline) | 75.2 s |
| `--maxWorkers=1` on the CLI | 76.6 s |
| `--maxWorkers=1` on the CLI, run via `node node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs` | 84.8 s |
| **`maxWorkers: 1` in the project's `test` config** | **139.0 s** |
| `--browser.fileParallelism=false` (for reference) | 129.7 s |

Every run passed 122/122 files. The config-file value lands within noise of `fileParallelism: false`, which is what serial execution should look like; the CLI value lands within noise of the baseline.

I ran it both through `bunx` and directly through `node` to rule out the package runner mangling the argument. Same result, so this is not a bun artefact.

### Where it seems to come from

The browser pool resolves its concurrency from the project config:

```js
// vitest/dist/chunks/cli-api.*.js (4.1.10)
function getThreadsCount(project) {
  const config = project.config.browser;
  if (!config.headless || !config.fileParallelism || !project.browser.provider.supportsParallelism) return 1;
  if (project.config.maxWorkers) return project.config.maxWorkers;
  return threadsCount;
}
```

That reads `project.config.maxWorkers`, and the pool then caps orchestrators at it. Given the measurements, the CLI-supplied value does not appear to reach `project.config.maxWorkers` for this project, so it falls through to the default. `--browser.fileParallelism=false` does reach the project, which is why that flag works.

Scope: I only tested a browser-mode project, so I do not know whether non-browser projects in a `projects` config are affected the same way, or whether this is specific to browser mode.

