Timeout errors lose their message: error.stack keeps the STACK_TRACE_ERROR placeholder header

### Describe the bug

When a test or hook times out, the resulting error's `stack` begins with `Error: STACK_TRACE_ERROR` instead of `Error: Test timed out in 60000ms...`.

`error.message` is correct. Only `error.stack` is affected, which matters because most reporters print or serialize `stack` in preference to `message`. The built-in JSON reporter populates `failureMessages` from the stack, so every timeout in a JSON report reads identically and gives no indication that a timeout occurred at all.

**Root cause**

`makeTimeoutError` in `@vitest/runner` (`dist/chunk-artifact.js:2394` in the built 4.1.2 package):

```js
function makeTimeoutError(isHook, timeout, stackTraceError) {
  const message = `${isHook ? "Hook" : "Test"} timed out in ${timeout}ms.\nIf this is a long-running ${isHook ? "hook" : "test"}, pass a timeout value as the last argument or configure it globally with "${isHook ? "hookTimeout" : "testTimeout"}".`;
  const error = new Error(message);
  if (stackTraceError?.stack) {
    error.stack = stackTraceError.stack.replace(error.message, stackTraceError.message);
  }
  return error;
}
```

`stackTraceError` is the placeholder captured at definition time (`new Error("STACK_TRACE_ERROR")`), whose stack carries the useful frames. That stack contains the string `STACK_TRACE_ERROR` and never contains the timeout message, so `.replace(error.message, stackTraceError.message)` matches nothing and returns the template stack unchanged. The assignment then overwrites the real header with the placeholder one.

The two arguments appear to be the wrong way round: the intent seems to be to put the timeout message onto the template's frames, which means replacing the placeholder text with the message rather than the message with the placeholder text.

**Suggested fix**

```diff
-    error.stack = stackTraceError.stack.replace(error.message, stackTraceError.message);
+    error.stack = stackTraceError.stack.replace(stackTraceError.message, error.message);
```

**Why it matters**

Tooling that keys on the stack cannot tell a timeout from any other failure, nor which budget was exceeded. In our case an automated nightly suite reported three consecutive failures as a bare `Error: STACK_TRACE_ERROR`, and identifying them as timeouts rather than assertion failures took several hours because the log carried no other signal.

I looked at the built output only; I have not checked whether the source in the repository differs.

### Reproduction

This is a pure string-manipulation bug, so it reproduces standalone with no vitest involved. Mirrors the function exactly:

```js
const tmpl = new Error("STACK_TRACE_ERROR");
const message = 'Test timed out in 60000ms.\nIf this is a long-running test, pass a timeout value as the last argument or configure it globally with "testTimeout".';

const error = new Error(message);
error.stack = tmpl.stack.replace(error.message, tmpl.message); // as shipped
console.log(error.stack.split("\n")[0]);
// -> Error: STACK_TRACE_ERROR

const fixed = new Error(message);
fixed.stack = tmpl.stack.replace(tmpl.message, fixed.message); // arguments swapped
console.log(fixed.stack.split("\n")[0]);
// -> Error: Test timed out in 60000ms.
```

In a real suite:

```js
import { it } from "vitest";

it("times out", async () => {
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200));
}, 10);
```

Run with `--reporter=json` and inspect `failureMessages[0]`. Its first line is `Error: STACK_TRACE_ERROR`, with no mention of a timeout or of the 10ms budget.

