Auto-created STI discriminator index is silently dropped when the entity has an embedded column

### Issue description

The index TypeORM auto-creates on a single-table-inheritance discriminator column is silently dropped when the STI base entity has at least one embedded column and at least one generated column.

### Expected Behavior

An STI base entity declared with `@TableInheritance()` gets an index on its discriminator column, as `EntityMetadataBuilder.createKeysForTableInheritance()` intends, regardless of whether the entity also has embedded columns.

### Actual Behavior

With an embedded column (`@Column(() => Something)`) present alongside a generated column (e.g. `@PrimaryGeneratedColumn()`), the discriminator index is missing from `EntityMetadata.indices` and is therefore never created in the schema. No error and no warning is emitted — `synchronize`/migration generation simply produces a table without that index.

Root cause is an array-identity subtlety in `src/metadata-builder/EntityMetadataBuilder.ts`.

`computeEntityMetadataStep2()` derives `indices` from `ownIndices`:

```ts
entityMetadata.indices = entityMetadata.embeddeds.reduce(
    (indices, embedded) => indices.concat(embedded.indicesFromTree),
    entityMetadata.ownIndices,
)
```

- With **zero** embeddeds, `Array.prototype.reduce` with an initial value and an empty array returns the initial value **by reference**, so `indices` *is* `ownIndices`.
- With **one or more** embeddeds, `concat` produces a **new** array, so `indices` is a copy that is no longer connected to `ownIndices`.

Later in `build()`, `createKeysForTableInheritance()` pushes the auto index onto `entityMetadata.indices`:

```ts
entityMetadata.indices.push(
    new IndexMetadata({ entityMetadata, columns: [entityMetadata.discriminatorColumn!], ... }),
)
```

In the zero-embedded case that push also lands in `ownIndices` (same array), so it survives. In the embedded case it lands only on the detached copy.

`build()` then re-runs `computeEntityMetadataStep2()` for every entity that has a generated column:

```ts
if (generated) {
    ...
    column.build(this.dataSource)
    this.computeEntityMetadataStep2(entityMetadata)
}
```

That re-run reassigns `indices` from `ownIndices` + embedded indices, and the auto-created discriminator index is discarded.

So the bug needs all three of: STI base, ≥1 embedded column, ≥1 generated column. A `@PrimaryGeneratedColumn()` id — which nearly every entity has — supplies the third.

An explicit `@Index()` on the discriminator column is unaffected: it comes from metadata args into `ownIndices`, so it is recreated by every step-2 run. That is also why the existing dedup guard in `createKeysForTableInheritance()` (`isDiscriminatorColumnAlreadyIndexed`) still behaves correctly once the underlying bug is fixed — no double index for users who already added the decorator by hand.

### Steps to reproduce

Minimal reproduction (`better-sqlite3`, in-memory — the outcome is decided at the metadata layer, so every driver is affected):

```ts
