`deepValue()` throws when find options omit an embedded that holds an eager relation

### Issue description

A query with `relationLoadStrategy: 'query'` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` when the entity has an eager relation declared inside an embedded, and the find options name some other relation without mentioning the embedded.

### Expected behaviour

The query returns the entities with the eager relation loaded, the same as it does when no find options are passed.

### Actual behaviour

`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'owner')`

### Steps to reproduce

```ts
import 'reflect-metadata';
import { Column, DataSource, Entity, ManyToOne, PrimaryGeneratedColumn } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
class User {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() name: string;
}

@Entity()
class Image {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() url: string;
}

class Meta {
    @ManyToOne(() => User, { eager: true, nullable: true })
    owner: User | null;
}

@Entity()
class Post {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn() id: number;
    @Column() title: string;
    @ManyToOne(() => Image, { nullable: true }) image: Image | null;
    @Column(() => Meta) meta: Meta;
}

async function main() {
    const dataSource = new DataSource({
        type: 'better-sqlite3',
        database: ':memory:',
        entities: [User, Image, Post],
        synchronize: true,
    });
    await dataSource.initialize();

    const user = await dataSource.getRepository(User).save({ name: 'Ann' });
    const image = await dataSource.getRepository(Image).save({ url: 'a.png' });
    await dataSource.getRepository(Post).save({ title: 'Hello', image, meta: { owner: user } });

    // The find options name `image`, and say nothing about the `meta` embedded which holds the
    // eager `owner` relation.
    const posts = await dataSource.getRepository(Post).find({
        relationLoadStrategy: 'query',
        relations: { image: true },
    });
    console.log('loaded', posts.length, 'post(s), owner =', posts[0].meta.owner?.name);
    await dataSource.destroy();
}

main().catch(e => {
    console.error(`${e.constructor.name}: ${e.message}`);
    process.exit(1);
});
```

On 1.1.0 this prints `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'owner')`. On 0.3.31 it prints `loaded 1 post(s), owner = Ann`.

### Cause

`OrmUtils.deepValue()` walks a dotted property path segment by segment without checking that the previous segment resolved to anything:

```js
static deepValue(obj, path) {
    const segments = path.split(".")
    for (let i = 0, len = segments.length; i < len; i++) {
        obj = obj[segments[i]]
    }
    return obj
}
```

`SelectQueryBuilder` calls it in seven places to narrow `select`, `order` and `relations` down to the part that applies to a single relation. All seven treat a missing path as "nothing specified for this relation". The call that brings the throw into an ordinary query is the one in the query-strategy branch of `executeEntitiesAndRawResults()`, which runs for every eager relation of the queried entity whenever find options are set:

```js
relations: this.findOptions.relations
    ? OrmUtils.deepValue(this.findOptions.relations, relation.propertyPath)
    : undefined,
```

For a relation declared inside an embedded, `relation.propertyPath` is `meta.owner`. Find options of `{ image: true }` have no `meta` key, so the loop reads `owner` of `undefined`.

0.3 ships the same `deepValue`. It does not reach this call with an embedded path, because its query strategy registers only the relations named in the find options and joins eager relations into the main query instead. The call that makes the path reachable arrived with #11326.

### Related

#10821 reports the same `deepValue` walking off the end of a path, reached through the `select` call sites rather than this one. It was closed by the stale-issue sweep in September 2025 without a fix, and the code it describes is unchanged.

