Relation object assigned inside an embedded entity is silently not persisted when the join column is shared with an explicit column

### Issue description

Assigning a relation object inside an embedded entity is silently not persisted when the join column is shared with an explicitly declared column (the "relation id column" pattern: `@Column() avatarId` alongside `@ManyToOne() avatar`). The identical pattern on a top-level (non-embedded) property works correctly.

### Expected Behavior

Given an embedded class with both a relation and its explicit id column:

```ts
export class Profile {
    @ManyToOne(() => Avatar)
    avatar: Avatar

    @Column({ type: "int", nullable: true })
    avatarId: number
}

@Entity()
export class User {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
    id: number

    @Column(() => Profile)
    profile: Profile
}
```

assigning the relation and saving:

```ts
user.profile.avatar = avatar
await dataSource.getRepository(User).save(user)
```

should persist the foreign key, so that a reload yields `loaded.profile.avatarId === avatar.id` — matching the behavior of the same pattern on a non-embedded property.

### Actual Behavior

The foreign key column is not written and remains `NULL`. No error is thrown and no SQL is generated for the column — the assignment is silently dropped, on both INSERT and UPDATE.

The cause is in `ColumnMetadata.getEntityValue()`. The embedded-column branch resolves the relation value like this:

```ts
const embeddedObject = extractEmbeddedColumnValue(propertyNames, entity)
if (embeddedObject) {
    if (this.relationMetadata && this.referencedColumn) {
        const relatedEntity =
            this.relationMetadata.getEntityValue(embeddedObject)
```

It passes the already-extracted `embeddedObject`. However, `RelationMetadata.getEntityValue()` expects the **full entity** — it performs the embedded-path traversal itself via `this.embeddedMetadata.parentPropertyNames`. The result is a double traversal (effectively `entity.profile.profile.avatar`), so `relatedEntity` always resolves to `undefined`, and the code falls back to `embeddedObject[this.propertyName]` (`avatarId`), which a relation-object assignment does not populate. The column value resolves to `undefined` and the persistence layer skips it.

The non-embedded branch of the same method correctly passes `entity`, which is why the top-level pattern works.

The same mismatch breaks the update path: `Subject.createValueSetAndPopChangeMap()` produces a value map keyed by the relation property (`{ profile: { avatar: { id: 1 } } }`), and the per-column value extraction via `getEntityValue()` fails to resolve it for the same reason.

Suggested fix — pass the full entity, since `RelationMetadata.getEntityValue()` handles the embedded traversal internally:

```ts
const relatedEntity = this.relationMetadata.getEntityValue(entity)
```

With this change, the reproduction below passes, and the full test suite (better-sqlite3) passes with no regressions. I will submit a PR with the fix and regression tests.

### Steps to reproduce

Reproduction in the repository's own test format (fails on current master):

```ts
