Sqlite: composite primary key ignored on synchronization

When using two columns as primary key in sqlite like this:
```typescript
import { Entity, PrimaryColumn, Column } from 'typeorm';

@Entity()
export class ExampleEntity {
  @PrimaryColumn()
  id1: string;

  @PrimaryColumn()
  id2: string;

  @Column()
  otherColumn: boolean;
}
```
When making a change a creation sql like this is generated:
```sql
CREATE TABLE "temporary_example_entity"
("id1" varchar NOT NULL, "id2" varchar NOT NULL, "otherColumn" boolean, PRIMARY KEY(id1))
```
But is should be
```sql
CREATE TABLE "temporary_example_entity"
("id1" varchar NOT NULL, "id2" varchar NOT NULL, "otherColumn" boolean, PRIMARY KEY(id1,id2))
```

This issue only exists when syncing. The initial creation is correct.

Support type-safe returns when using transactions

Currently there is no way to return "type-safe" data from inside of a transaction in a "non-hackish" way. That's because signature of the transaction method always returns ``Promise<any>``. The two possible workaround that I've seen are:
* Assign values to variables in outer scope (use by existing test cases in TypeORM repository, eg. this one: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/test/functional/transaction/transaction-in-entity-manager/transaction-in-entity-manager.ts#L34)
    * Mentioned snippet:
    ```typescript
    let postId: number|undefined = undefined, categoryId: number|undefined = undefined;

    await connection.manager.transaction(async entityManager => {

        const post = new Post();
        post.title = "Post #1";
        await entityManager.save(post);

        const category = new Category();
        category.name = "Category #1";
        await entityManager.save(category);

        postId = post.id;
        categoryId = category.id;

    });
    ```
    * Obviously, it sucks that we have to allow ``undefined`` being assigned to ``postId`` because it is late-bound by the transaction. We should be able to catch it using ``const``.
* Unsafe typecasting from ``Promise<any>`` to our desired type.
    * Example:  
    ```typescript
    const postId = await (await connection.manager.transaction(async entityManager => {

        const post = new Post();
        post.title = "Post #1";
        await entityManager.save(post);

        return post.id;

    })) as number;
    ```
    * If someone changes what type ``post.id`` uses, this code will silently break, because we are forcing TS to use ``number`` type.

It's kind of strange that ``transaction`` forces to use ``Promise<any>`` when there are generics and type inference working pretty well in TypeScript. We should be able to do something like this:
```typescript
// Type inference should guess what is returned by the arrow function
// If needed, we could also define returning type ourselves
const { postId, categoryId } = await connection.manager.transaction(async entityManager => {

    const post = new Post();
    post.title = "Post #1";
    await entityManager.save(post);

    const category = new Category();
    category.name = "Category #1";
    await entityManager.save(category);

    return {
        postId: post.id,
        categoryId: category.id
    };

});

// Types of "postId" and "categoryId" are known at "compile-time"
```
